I wasn't clear about that, but I meant to include relative paths. The std is a good point, I checked what cargo does, as I was sure it includes paths in the passed metadata. It special cases the standard library when computing the relative path:

```rust
    let ws_root = if unit.is_std {
        // SourceId for stdlib crates is an absolute path inside the sysroot.
        // Pass the sysroot as workspace root so that we hash a relative path.
        // This avoids the metadata hash changing depending on where the user installed rustc.
        &bcx.target_data.get_info(unit.kind).unwrap().sysroot
    } else {
        bcx.ws.root()
    };

    // Unique metadata per (name, source,, version) triple. This'll allow us
    // to pull crates from anywhere without worrying about conflicts.
    unit.pkg
        .package_id()
        .stable_hash(ws_root)
        .hash(&mut shared_hasher);
```
Using the same relative paths should solve any incompatibilities with cargo.

I am not going to implement this for now and document the public api hash collisions of transitive dependencies living on different paths as a source of unsoundness.  And that it is unlikely to occur, since it contains and will keep containing the `StableCrateId`.
It can be decided later what is an acceptable amount of risk here. The hash size can be increased, the full `-Cmetadata` can be checked, while documenting that build systems should check for collisions. Or a similar relative path can be saved, just like cargo does it.

